WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

00:00.320 --> 00:02.129
In this lesson, you'll learn

00:02.129 --> 00:03.859
how you can use your Pydantic data models

00:03.859 --> 00:06.740
directly in your API call to an LLM provider.

00:06.740 --> 00:09.140
And what you'll see is that this gives you

00:09.140 --> 00:11.960
a much cleaner and more reliable way to get a structured response

00:11.960 --> 00:15.060
than what we were doing in the last lesson

00:15.060 --> 00:16.803
of all that error handling and

00:16.803 --> 00:18.838
retry logic that you had to build.

00:18.838 --> 00:21.899
And what you're going to do here is to play with this

00:21.899 --> 00:23.469
across a number of different frameworks

00:23.469 --> 00:25.588
and LLM providers.

00:25.588 --> 00:27.910
And with that, you'll get firsthand experience

00:27.910 --> 00:29.996
with the fact that it kind of doesn't matter

00:29.996 --> 00:32.207
which LLM provider you're working with

00:32.207 --> 00:33.840
or which agent framework. Really, the

00:33.840 --> 00:35.746
way in which you're going to use

00:35.746 --> 00:38.277
pydantic data models to get the structure that you want

00:38.277 --> 00:40.337
in the response is essentially the same

00:40.856 --> 00:43.267
across different LLMs and agent frameworks.

00:44.036 --> 00:46.237
So, let's jump into the notebook and see how that works.

00:47.826 --> 00:49.417
All right, so to start off with,

00:49.417 --> 00:51.006
you're adding a couple of imports here.

00:51.557 --> 00:54.786
You're importing instructor, which you can use as a wrapper

00:54.786 --> 00:57.017
around a number of different LLM providers

00:57.017 --> 00:59.844
in the API call. to use your pydantic data model

00:59.844 --> 01:02.353
in the API call to get a structured response.

01:02.353 --> 01:04.414
And the way instructor works is actually

01:04.414 --> 01:06.733
quite similar to what you were doing in the last lesson.

01:07.153 --> 01:08.953
You pass in the pydantic data model.

01:08.953 --> 01:11.084
instructor extracts the JSON model schema,

01:11.084 --> 01:13.563
and then constructs a prompt from that.

01:14.013 --> 01:16.114
And if there's any issue in the response,

01:16.114 --> 01:18.289
it will do a series of retries in order

01:18.289 --> 01:20.463
to get you back the response you're looking for.

01:20.813 --> 01:22.180
And you're going to use that

01:22.180 --> 01:23.774
with anthropic to start off this lesson.

01:26.024 --> 01:29.081
The next thing you can do is Let's go ahead and define

01:29.081 --> 01:32.492
the pydantic data models you're familiar with from the previous lesson.

01:32.492 --> 01:34.171
So you have the same UserInput model

01:34.171 --> 01:36.492
here and the same CustomerQuery model

01:36.492 --> 01:38.772
that inherits from UserInput.

01:38.772 --> 01:40.382
You can go ahead and define those.

01:42.091 --> 01:45.042
And then you can create some example user input data.

01:45.042 --> 01:47.212
So here you have a user_input_json,

01:47.212 --> 01:50.191
just like you did before with information in all the fields.

01:51.941 --> 01:53.861
And you can validate that user_input

01:53.861 --> 01:56.522
by using your model_validate_json method.

01:56.659 --> 02:01.123
on the input. And then you can construct a prompt.

02:01.834 --> 02:03.579
You're going to make a really simple prompt

02:03.579 --> 02:05.323
that just says analyze the following customer query,

02:05.504 --> 02:09.304
passing in that user input, and provide a structured response.

02:10.717 --> 02:13.646
And then you can go ahead and set up an LLM call.

02:13.817 --> 02:16.396
In this case, using instructor

02:16.947 --> 02:19.092
in combination with anthropic to create a

02:19.092 --> 02:21.546
client where you can ask for a response

02:21.866 --> 02:25.456
and pass in your prompt as well as a response model.

02:25.537 --> 02:27.747
in this case your CustomerQuery model

02:27.747 --> 02:29.947
as the response format that you're looking for.

02:29.947 --> 02:32.417
So with instructor, you could do this with OpenAI

02:32.417 --> 02:34.447
or Gemini or many of the other

02:34.447 --> 02:36.766
LLM providers out there, but just to change

02:36.766 --> 02:39.417
things up a little bit, we're going to try this with Anthropic.

02:40.926 --> 02:43.187
And then you can have a look at what you got back.

02:45.997 --> 02:47.567
So in this case, what you got back

02:47.567 --> 02:50.537
is actually an instance of your CustomerQuery data model.

02:50.537 --> 02:52.431
So the Instructor package has gone

02:52.431 --> 02:54.644
off, made the LLM call for you,

02:54.644 --> 02:58.343
and done any retrying that was necessary, and then also

02:58.343 --> 03:01.443
validated the data and passed you back a populated

03:01.443 --> 03:04.044
instance of your CustomerQuery data model.

03:04.044 --> 03:05.843
So there's no more need for

03:05.843 --> 03:07.644
extra validation steps on your end.

03:07.644 --> 03:09.258
You get back a valid data

03:09.258 --> 03:11.144
model with all the fields filled out.

03:11.144 --> 03:13.843
And so you could use instructor just like

03:13.843 --> 03:16.544
this with a number of other LLM providers,

03:16.544 --> 03:18.722
but there are also ways you can do this

03:18.722 --> 03:21.144
directly with an API call to Open AI, for example.

03:21.144 --> 03:23.751
So here what you're doing is

03:23.751 --> 03:25.531
creating an OpenAI client

03:25.531 --> 03:27.991
and then calling the beta API

03:27.991 --> 03:31.781
with a response format of CustomerQuery, your data model.

03:31.781 --> 03:34.700
And then you're going to grab the content of the response.

03:34.700 --> 03:36.366
and print out the type of response you're

03:36.366 --> 03:38.031
getting back as well as what's in it.

03:39.401 --> 03:40.661
And when you do that, what you can

03:40.661 --> 03:41.920
see is that you're getting back a string,

03:41.920 --> 03:45.031
just like you would in a normal LLM response,

03:45.031 --> 03:47.575
and that the format of that string looks

03:47.575 --> 03:50.441
to be approximately a representation of your data model.

03:50.441 --> 03:52.859
And so what's different here from what

03:52.859 --> 03:55.618
you just did with instructor and Anthropic is that

03:55.618 --> 03:59.058
you're not getting back a validated instance of your data model directly,

03:59.058 --> 04:01.729
but you're getting back a JSON string,

04:01.729 --> 04:04.308
and what OpenAI is doing behind the scenes here

04:04.308 --> 04:06.449
is something called constrained generation,

04:06.449 --> 04:08.575
which is to say because you've

04:08.575 --> 04:11.058
indicated that you're looking for JSON format

04:11.058 --> 04:14.254
in the response, the way that the response is actually

04:14.254 --> 04:16.718
generated guarantees that you'll get back valid JSON.

04:16.718 --> 04:18.709
Now that's not to say that this is guaranteed

04:18.709 --> 04:20.699
to be a valid instance of your data model,

04:20.699 --> 04:23.105
but it is guaranteed to be valid JSON in this case.

04:23.105 --> 04:25.817
You're never going to have to deal with any kind of

04:25.817 --> 04:28.776
malformed JSON in the response when you're using OpenAI in this way.

04:29.235 --> 04:32.050
And what you can do next is to go ahead and try

04:32.050 --> 04:33.665
to create an instance of your CustomerQuery

04:33.665 --> 04:37.615
data model from that response_content using the model_validate_json method.

04:37.855 --> 04:40.031
and then print out what you get

04:40.031 --> 04:42.206
back. So in this case it worked.

04:42.206 --> 04:43.786
You got back valid data

04:43.786 --> 04:46.966
and didn't run into any validation errors in populating your data model.

04:47.136 --> 04:48.484
But it was one extra step to

04:48.484 --> 04:50.026
get to a valid instance of your model.

04:50.456 --> 04:53.074
It turns out that that OpenAI also offers

04:53.074 --> 04:56.983
another version of their API, this responses API,

04:56.983 --> 05:00.064
where you can pass in a parameter called text_format

05:00.064 --> 05:02.303
and put your Pydantic data model in there.

05:02.303 --> 05:03.944
And then when you run this,

05:03.944 --> 05:06.343
we'll take a look at what kind of response you get back.

05:07.473 --> 05:09.353
So in this case, you can see that it's some kind of

05:09.353 --> 05:13.153
class, uh, with some nesting structure

05:13.153 --> 05:14.647
and deep down inside of there, it

05:14.647 --> 05:16.353
looks like you have an instance of your

05:16.353 --> 05:17.874
CustomerQuery data model.

05:18.613 --> 05:20.180
And what I think is really interesting to

05:20.180 --> 05:22.081
do at this point before

05:22.081 --> 05:25.341
digging into what actually is inside of that response

05:25.341 --> 05:28.180
is to look at that response structure a little bit more closely.

05:28.180 --> 05:31.261
So here you're just defining a little function to

05:31.261 --> 05:33.221
pass in that LLM response and

05:33.221 --> 05:34.872
you don't need to worry about the

05:34.872 --> 05:36.761
syntax that's going on here, but this .mro()

05:36.761 --> 05:41.091
is what's known as method resolution order in Python and that'll pull

05:41.091 --> 05:44.990
just allow you to print out the inheritance structure of that response.

05:44.990 --> 05:46.860
And so when you run that,

05:46.860 --> 05:49.288
what you can see is at

05:49.288 --> 05:51.437
the top level, you have this nested structure,

05:51.437 --> 05:53.958
and if you unpack that all the way down to the bottom,

05:53.958 --> 05:57.028
you eventually get to a pydantic.main.BaseModel.

05:57.028 --> 06:00.278
And so, it's very interesting to note that the response

06:00.278 --> 06:04.307
coming back to you from OpenAI is itself a pydantic model.

06:04.307 --> 06:05.726
And what you've got in this case

06:05.726 --> 06:07.348
is an instance of your own pydantic model

06:07.348 --> 06:10.228
nested deep inside another pydantic model from OpenAI.

06:10.228 --> 06:12.918
And it turns out that this is very common.

06:12.918 --> 06:15.528
A lot of LLM providers out there

06:15.528 --> 06:18.396
are returning a pydantic data model to you.

06:18.396 --> 06:21.773
as the response type. And then deep down inside that model

06:21.773 --> 06:23.495
somewhere is the content that you're interested in.

06:24.076 --> 06:25.786
And it's at this point that I think you can

06:25.786 --> 06:27.495
start to see that when it comes to LLM workflows,

06:27.495 --> 06:30.295
it's kind of pydantic models all the way down.

06:30.786 --> 06:32.430
You've got pydantic models that you're passing

06:32.430 --> 06:34.076
around to get the structure you want.

06:34.625 --> 06:38.255
The responses that are coming back many times are pydantic models themselves,

06:38.495 --> 06:40.081
and that's because these LLM providers are

06:40.081 --> 06:41.896
doing their own data validation on their end

06:41.896 --> 06:43.896
before sending you back a response.

06:44.185 --> 06:45.841
And then as you'll see in the next lesson,

06:45.841 --> 06:47.495
you'll also be using pydantic data models and tools.

06:47.502 --> 06:49.927
calls. So for me at least, I think

06:49.927 --> 06:51.463
this is kind of cool to discover how

06:51.463 --> 06:55.403
pydantic models are just woven through and through in LLM workflows.

06:56.002 --> 06:57.588
And so, let's take a look at

06:57.588 --> 06:59.403
what you actually got back in this case.

07:00.192 --> 07:03.497
When you're using the responses API from OpenAI then

07:03.497 --> 07:06.802
the content you're looking for is inside of response.output_parsed.

07:07.853 --> 07:09.617
And so you can see inside of there,

07:09.617 --> 07:11.603
you have an instance of your CustomerQuery data model,

07:11.603 --> 07:14.302
which means that the API has returned valid data,

07:14.302 --> 07:16.502
and there's no more validation required on your end.

07:16.971 --> 07:19.201
And so in this case, OpenAI is handling the construction of the

07:19.201 --> 07:23.500
initial JSON using constrained generation.

07:23.500 --> 07:25.858
And also populating the data model for you,

07:25.858 --> 07:28.511
handling any validation before handing it back to you.

07:29.000 --> 07:31.620
And finally, I feel like this lesson would not be complete

07:32.031 --> 07:34.670
if we didn't take a look at pydantic_ai, which is

07:35.110 --> 07:37.971
an agent framework built by the folks at Pydantic,

07:38.290 --> 07:40.240
where you can create an agent like this,

07:40.471 --> 07:43.380
pass in your data model as the output_type

07:43.571 --> 07:45.521
and it's just very simple to

07:45.718 --> 07:49.538
set up an LLM call to really just about any LLM provider.

07:49.538 --> 07:50.937
In this case, just for fun, we're

07:50.937 --> 07:52.338
going to go ahead and call Gemini.

07:52.497 --> 07:55.187
And this nest_asyncio stuff, you don't need to worry about.

07:55.187 --> 07:57.237
This is just so that pydantic_ai

07:57.237 --> 07:59.627
plays well within the Jupyter notebook environment.

07:59.997 --> 08:03.218
So let's run that. And then look at what you get back.

08:04.838 --> 08:07.517
So in this case again, in response.output,

08:07.517 --> 08:10.817
you've got an instance of your CustomerQuery data model.

08:10.817 --> 08:14.017
and so you've got valid data coming back directly in the response.

08:14.418 --> 08:17.129
And so you can see that there are some differences in the

08:17.129 --> 08:19.725
responses that you're getting back, namely the tags field

08:19.725 --> 08:21.795
has different tags in each case, and that's

08:21.795 --> 08:23.865
just due to the fact that that's a

08:23.865 --> 08:26.038
list of strings that each LLM provider

08:26.038 --> 08:28.524
is going to populate a little bit differently.

08:29.064 --> 08:32.975
And in this case with pydantic_ai, you could go ahead and

08:32.975 --> 08:36.884
switch to another model provider quite easily, like openai for example.

08:40.745 --> 08:45.413
Try calling openai:gpt-4o. And that worked.

08:46.082 --> 08:48.893
And you can see that you get back a slightly different response,

08:48.893 --> 08:51.038
but all you have to do is change out

08:51.038 --> 08:53.182
this model string to call a different LLM provider.

08:53.702 --> 08:55.212
And so here you've seen how you can

08:55.212 --> 08:56.913
pass your Pydantic data model directly in the prompt

08:56.913 --> 08:59.177
to get structured output with a lot less hassle

08:59.177 --> 09:01.442
than you were dealing with in the last lesson.

09:02.173 --> 09:04.346
So in this lesson, you've been using

09:04.346 --> 09:06.832
Pydantic data models directly in your API calls

09:06.832 --> 09:09.633
to different LLM providers and agent frameworks.

09:09.633 --> 09:11.220
And you've seen that this can

09:11.220 --> 09:13.072
be an effective way of getting structured

09:13.200 --> 09:15.659
output and validated data from an LLM.

09:16.360 --> 09:18.899
Next up, we're going to take a look at tool calling,

09:19.049 --> 09:22.419
which is the other big use case for pydantic models

09:22.419 --> 09:26.950
within LLM workflows. So in the next lesson, you'll use pydantic models

09:27.509 --> 09:29.185
in the definition of tools that you'll

09:29.185 --> 09:31.100
pass to an LLM in an API call.

09:31.610 --> 09:33.602
And that will allow the LLM to

09:33.602 --> 09:35.879
give you back the exact parameters you need

09:36.509 --> 09:39.029
to call the tool that uses a function or an API

09:39.269 --> 09:41.289
as part of your system. I'll see you there.